Alice has finished a magical tiered cake with n magical layers, where each layer is smaller than all layers below it (i.e. the first layer is the smallest, the n-th layer is the largest). Now, she needs your help to transport it from her kitchen to the party site. Since moving the whole cake at once is impossible, she also prepares a warehouse for you to ease the transportation process.
Since the cake is magical, the i-th layer of cake is movable if and only if there are exactly ai layers of cake above it.
In each move, you can choose exactly one movable layer of cake from any location and move it on top of the tiered cake in any other location. However, to preserve a structure of the cake, the moved layer has to be on the layer that is strictly larger than it if there is a tiered cake at the destination. For example, you cannot move a layer with size 4 on top of a location where there is already a layer with size 3.
The party is starting soon, so we need to move fast. Help Alice transport the cake to the party within 2n moves or report that it is impossible.

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1≤t≤10000). The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each test case contains an integer n (1≤n≤20) – the number of layers in the magical tiered cake.
The second line of each test case contains n integers a1,a2,…,an (0≤ai≤n), which represent how many layers of cake need to be above the i-th layer for it to be movable.
It is guaranteed that the sum of 2n across all test cases does not exceed 220.

Output:
For each test case, if it is impossible to move the magical tiered cake to the party site, output NO.
Otherwise, on the first line, output YES.
Then, on the next line, output an integer k (0≤k≤2n) – the number of moves you make to transport the cake.
For the following k lines, output integers id, from, to (1≤id≤n,1≤from,to≤3): the layer of cake you want to move, its current location, and its destination, where 1 represents Alice's kitchen, 2 represents Alice's warehouse, and 3 represents the party site.

Strategy:
This is a tower of hanoi problem. The normal problem is solving with recursion. We can have a base case (i.e. layer < 0) and return from there. For all of the other ones we can save the moves inside a log array. In the normal tower of hanoi we can solve just by using solve(layer-1, source, aux) op.push_back(layer, source, target) and solve(layer-1, aux, target). This way at the end of each move we end up with the full tower rearranged at one of the 3 spots. The only thing that changed in this problem is that each layer has a set amount of layers that need to be above for it to be pulled out from its place in the cake. Let the count of layers needed to be above layer i to be moved be ai. This changes the problem to moving i-ai-1 (0 indexed) away, leaving ai layers so we can move the layer out. This can be done the exactly same way as normal tower of hanoi. Let excess=i-ai-1 We just add a branch to check if ai>0 and if it is we run solve(excess, source, aux), op.push_back(layer, source, target), solve(excess, aux, source) and solve(layer-1, source, target). This way we always end up with the tower, again, fully composed at the target. This, just as the normal tower of hanoi recursively decomposes into moving smaller and smaller towers to the target which become the building blocks for lower layers. We go bottom up since thats how you solve even a normal problem, you move the biggest layer and go up. We also need to check if its even possible. This is easy since the tower has to be possible to recreate if each layer is at a valid position at the start meaning its ai is <= i. We check for that with a basic loop. We start with solve(n-1, 1, 3) this attempts to move the whole tower based at layer n-1 (0 indexed) to the third position (target). We print the size of the op array (vector of vectors) and then for each element we print the inner vector with the first element+1 since we need to make it 1 indexed.